assertion  XSH.llrint.001
anchor     functions/llrint.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/llrint.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      2001
says       While the rounding direction is toward negative infinity, toward
           positive infinity or toward zero (each one that can be selected; at
           least one is needed), llrint() of -3.5, -2.75, -2.5, -1.5, -1.25,
           -0.75, 0.75, 1.25, 1.5, 2.5, 2.75 and 3.5 gives the integer below,
           above or toward zero respectively.
test       t/001-directed.c

assertion  XSH.llrint.002
anchor     functions/llrint.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/llrint.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      2001
says       While the rounding direction is to nearest, halfway cases go to the
           even neighbour: llrint(0.5) is 0, llrint(1.5) is 2, llrint(2.5) is 2,
           llrint(3.5) is 4, llrint(4.5) is 4 and the negative counterparts;
           other values go to the nearest integer.
note       The tag on this passage is the IEC 60559 Floating-Point option; the
           test is NOTINUSE unless the C implementation claims it (Annex F of the
           C standard). The test is NOTINUSE when to-nearest cannot be selected.
test       t/002-nearest-even.c

assertion  XSH.llrint.003
anchor     functions/llrint.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/llrint.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      2001
says       llrint() of 0, -0, 5, -5, 1048576 and -1048576 returns 0, 0, 5, -5,
           1048576 and -1048576.
test       t/003-integral.c

assertion  XSH.llrint.004
anchor     functions/llrint.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/llrint.html#RETURN VALUE
anchor     functions/llrint.html#ERRORS
class      required
since      2001
says       A NaN argument is a domain error: errno is EDOM when it is in use for
           errors, and the invalid exception is raised when exception flags are
           in use.
note       The tag on this passage is the IEC 60559 Floating-Point option; the
           test is NOTINUSE unless the C implementation claims it (Annex F of the
           C standard).
test       t/004-nan.c

assertion  XSH.llrint.005
anchor     functions/llrint.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/llrint.html#RETURN VALUE
anchor     functions/llrint.html#ERRORS
class      required
since      2001
says       +Inf and -Inf arguments are domain errors, reported through errno
           (EDOM) and the invalid exception as math_errhandling selects.
note       The tag on this passage is the IEC 60559 Floating-Point option; the
           test is NOTINUSE unless the C implementation claims it (Annex F of the
           C standard).
test       t/005-infinity.c

assertion  XSH.llrint.006
anchor     functions/llrint.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/llrint.html#RETURN VALUE
anchor     functions/llrint.html#ERRORS
class      required
since      2001
says       For 2^w, with w the width of long long in bits (far above its largest
           value), a domain error occurs and is reported as math_errhandling
           selects.
note       The tag on this passage is the IEC 60559 Floating-Point option; the
           test is NOTINUSE unless the C implementation claims it (Annex F of the
           C standard).
test       t/006-too-large-positive.c

assertion  XSH.llrint.007
anchor     functions/llrint.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/llrint.html#RETURN VALUE
anchor     functions/llrint.html#ERRORS
class      required
since      2001
says       For -2^w, with w the width of long long in bits (far below its
           smallest value), a domain error occurs and is reported as
           math_errhandling selects.
note       The tag on this passage is the IEC 60559 Floating-Point option; the
           test is NOTINUSE unless the C implementation claims it (Annex F of the
           C standard).
test       t/007-too-large-negative.c

assertion  XSH.llrint.008
anchor     functions/llrint.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/llrint.html#RETURN VALUE
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap04.html#4.23
class      required
since      2001
says       With errno holding EDOM before the call, llrint(2.5) leaves it
           unchanged and, when exception flags are in use, raises none of the
           invalid, divide-by-zero and overflow exceptions.
test       t/008-no-error.c
